  "summary": "Ink blots and bleach-white faces: crime comics reduced to pure light and dark.",
  "description": "The high-contrast noir comic — canonized by Frank Miller's Sin City — draws with absence: massive black ink masses, few interior lines, spotlight-white faces and the occasional single accent color (the red dress, the yellow bastard). The tradition descends from Silver Streak horror and film-noir stills but was Miller's Sin City (1991) that made posterized chiaroscuro a working method: brush-drawn blacks as architecture, hatchless shadows, mood carried entirely by value structure. Eddie Campbell, Sean Phillips (Criminal) and Sean Murphy work the dialect; the style's digital descendant is the high-contrast webcomic frame, and its film adaptation (2005) moved the look to screen almost unchanged.",
  "culturalContext": "Sin City made posterized chiaroscuro a working method: Miller's ink-mass noir spawned a crime-comic school (Phillips's Criminal), translated unchanged to film, and remains the reference for value-structure storytelling in comics education.",
